  ←→  (⍺ f ⍵) g (⍺ h ⍵)\n```\n\nBoth outer tines see the original argument(s); the middle one combines their results. This is the workhorse, and it's why trains read like math notation:\n\n```apl\nmean ← +⌿ ÷ ≢          ⍝ sum ÷ count\nmean 1 2 3 4           ⍝ 2.5\n\nrange   ← ⌈/ - ⌊/\ndevs    ← ⊢ - +⌿÷≢     ⍝ 5-train: ⍵ - mean ⍵\npalin   ← ⌽ ≡ ⊢\n```\n\nDyadic forks are where `⊢`/`⊣` earn their keep:\n\n```apl\n' ' (≠⊆⊢) 'hello there world'   ⍝ (⍺≠⍵) ⊆ ⍵  →  split on blanks\n3   (⌊,⌈) 7                     ⍝ (3⌊7),(3⌈7)  →  3 7\n```\n\n## Longer trains\n\nGroup from the right in threes. An odd-length train is nested forks; an even-length one is an atop over that:\n\n```apl\nf g h j      ←→  f (g h j)\nf g h j k    ←→  f g (h j k)\na b c d e f g ←→ a b (c d (e f g))\n```\n\nSo `⊢ - +⌿ ÷ ≢` is `fork(⊢, -, fork(+⌿, ÷, ≢))`.\n\n## Rules worth internalizing\n\n**A train must be isolated.** Parentheses or assignment create one; otherwise the rightmost function just eats the argument and you get a plain right-to-left chain:\n\n```apl\n(+⌿÷≢) ⍳10    ⍝ 5.5  — fork\n +⌿÷≢  ⍳10    ⍝ sum of reciprocals of ⍳10 — not a train\n```\n\n**An array may be the left tine of a fork**, acting as a constant:\n\n```apl\n(0 = 2∘|) 7    ⍝ hmm — but e.g.\n2 | ⊢          ⍝ 2 | ⍵\n```\n\nThat only works in the leftmost position of a 3-train; `(2 ×)` alone is a syntax error in Dyalog — use `2∘×`.\n\n**J's 2-train is a hook, not an atop.** This trips people moving between dialects:\n\n```j\n(f g) y    NB.  y f (g y)      J hook\nx (f g) y  NB.  x f (g y)\n```\n\nDyalog, BQN, and ngn/k-style APLs use atop for the 2-train; J uses the hook (BQN spells the hook explicitly as `⊸`/`⟜`).\n\n## The mental model\n\nA fork is \"run two pipelines over the same data, then combine.\" Once you see that shape, a lot of array code stops being a chain of steps and becomes a single expression you can name, pass to an operator, or invert.\n\nIf you're heading toward q: it has no trains. The equivalent is an explicit lambda (`{(sum x)%count x}`) or `'[f;g]` composition, so fork-shaped APL usually lands as a lambda that mentions `x` twice.","ttft_ms":9877,"ttft_stream_ms":834,"time_to_request_ms":23,"type":"result","duration_ms":93448,"uuid":"52e9b84b-7320-4826-82df-3ed715aa7a89"}
